window.ND=window.ND||{};Object.assign(ND,{"cn|Synapse":{"d":"## Definition and types\nA synapse is the functional junction between two neurons (or neuron–effector). Types:\n- **Chemical** (most common) – unidirectional, has synaptic delay.\n- **Electrical** – gap junctions (connexons); bidirectional, no delay; e.g. retina, inferior olive, cardiac muscle, smooth muscle.\n- Anatomical: axodendritic (commonest, mostly excitatory), axosomatic (often inhibitory), axoaxonic (presynaptic inhibition).\n\n## Structure\nPresynaptic terminal (bouton) with **vesicles** and mitochondria; **synaptic cleft ≈ 20–30 nm**; postsynaptic membrane with receptors (postsynaptic density).\n\n## Transmission\nAction potential → opening of voltage-gated **Ca²⁺ channels** → Ca²⁺ influx → vesicle docking and fusion (**SNARE proteins** – synaptobrevin, syntaxin, SNAP-25; synaptotagmin is Ca²⁺ sensor) → exocytosis (quantal release) → transmitter binds receptors:\n- **Ionotropic** (ligand-gated channels) – fast (nicotinic, GABA-A, glycine, AMPA/NMDA).\n- **Metabotropic** (GPCR) – slow, modulatory (muscarinic, adrenergic, GABA-B, mGluR, dopamine).\nTermination – enzymatic breakdown (AChE), **reuptake** (amines, glutamate, GABA), diffusion.\n**Botulinum** and **tetanus toxins** cleave SNAREs.\n\n## Postsynaptic potentials\n- **EPSP** – opening of Na⁺ (and K⁺) cation channels → depolarisation ≈ 0.5–1 mV per input; duration ≈ 15 ms. Glutamate is the main excitatory transmitter.\n- **IPSP** – Cl⁻ influx or K⁺ efflux → hyperpolarisation (towards ≈ −70 to −80 mV); transmitters GABA (brain) and glycine (spinal cord).\n- Graded, non-propagated, can summate. AP is generated at the **axon hillock/initial segment** (lowest threshold – highest Na⁺ channel density).\n\n## Properties of synaptic transmission\n1. **One-way conduction** (Bell–Magendie law) – transmitter only in presynaptic terminal.\n2. **Synaptic delay** ≈ **0.5 ms** per synapse – used to count synapses in a reflex arc.\n3. **Summation** – **spatial** (several terminals simultaneously) and **temporal** (repeated firing of one terminal).\n4. **Fatigue** – depletion of transmitter with repeated stimulation (protective; e.g. after epileptic fit).\n5. **Facilitation** and **post-tetanic potentiation** (↑residual Ca²⁺) → basis of memory; **long-term potentiation (LTP)** in hippocampus (NMDA receptors).\n6. **Convergence** and **divergence**; **occlusion**; **subliminal fringe**; reverberation (after-discharge).\n7. **Sensitivity** to hypoxia, acidosis (↓excitability – coma) and alkalosis (↑excitability – seizures, as in hyperventilation); drugs (caffeine ↑, anaesthetics ↓).\n\n## Inhibition\n- **Postsynaptic** – IPSP (direct; e.g. reciprocal inhibition via glycinergic interneurons; **Renshaw cell recurrent inhibition**).\n- **Presynaptic** – axoaxonic synapse (GABA) reduces Ca²⁺ entry and transmitter release in a specific input (e.g. sensory pathway gating).\n- Feed-forward and feedback inhibition, lateral inhibition (sharpens sensation).\n\n## Neurotransmitters (examples)\nACh, noradrenaline, dopamine, serotonin, histamine; amino acids (glutamate, aspartate, GABA, glycine); peptides (substance P, enkephalins, endorphins, NPY); gases (NO – retrograde messenger); ATP, adenosine; endocannabinoids. **Dale's principle** – a neuron releases the same transmitter(s) at all its terminals (co-transmission common).\n\n## Applied\nMyasthenia gravis (neuromuscular junction), strychnine (blocks glycine receptors → convulsions), tetanus toxin (blocks glycine/GABA release from Renshaw/inhibitory neurons → spastic paralysis, lockjaw), benzodiazepines (↑GABA-A), Parkinson's (dopamine loss), SSRIs (serotonin reuptake), epilepsy.","q":{"b":["Chemical synapse – one-way, delay; electrical – gap junctions.","Synaptic cleft ≈ 20–30 nm.","Ca²⁺ influx triggers vesicle exocytosis (SNAREs).","Botulinum/tetanus toxins cleave SNAREs.","EPSP – depolarisation; IPSP – Cl⁻/K⁺ hyperpolarisation.","AP starts at axon hillock.","Synaptic delay ≈ 0.5 ms.","Spatial and temporal summation.","Fatigue = transmitter depletion.","LTP (NMDA) underlies memory.","Presynaptic inhibition via axoaxonic GABA synapse.","Renshaw cells – recurrent inhibition (glycine).","Alkalosis ↑, acidosis ↓ synaptic excitability."],"n":["Cleft 20–30 nm","Delay 0.5 ms","EPSP ≈ 15 ms","IPSP toward −70 to −80 mV"],"m":"Synapse properties **\"ODS-FFC\"** – One-way, Delay, Summation, Fatigue, Facilitation, Convergence/divergence.","v":["Ion triggering transmitter release? – Ca²⁺.","Synaptic delay? – ≈ 0.5 ms.","Mechanism of strychnine poisoning? – blocks glycine receptors."]}},"cn|Sensory receptors":{"d":"## Definition\nReceptors are specialised transducers converting stimulus energy into **receptor (generator) potentials** and then action potentials in afferent nerves.\n\n## Classification\n- By stimulus (**adequate stimulus** – the form of energy to which a receptor is most sensitive):\n - **Mechanoreceptors** – touch, pressure, vibration, hearing, balance, baroreceptors, stretch (muscle spindle).\n - **Thermoreceptors** – warm and cold (TRPV, TRPM8 channels).\n - **Nociceptors** – pain (free nerve endings).\n - **Chemoreceptors** – taste, smell, carotid body, osmoreceptors.\n - **Photoreceptors** (electromagnetic) – rods and cones.\n- By location (Sherrington): **exteroceptors**, **interoceptors** (visceroceptors), **proprioceptors**, telereceptors (vision, hearing, smell).\n- By adaptation: **rapidly adapting (phasic)** – Pacinian, Meissner's, hair follicle (detect change/velocity); **slowly adapting (tonic)** – Merkel's discs, Ruffini endings, muscle spindle, Golgi tendon organ, nociceptors, baroreceptors (detect continuous stimulus).\n\n## Cutaneous mechanoreceptors\n- **Meissner's corpuscles** – glabrous skin (fingertips, lips), fine touch, flutter, two-point discrimination; rapidly adapting.\n- **Merkel's discs** – sustained touch, texture, pressure; slowly adapting; high resolution.\n- **Pacinian corpuscles** – deep, onion-like lamellae; **vibration (≈ 200–300 Hz)**; very rapidly adapting.\n- **Ruffini endings** – skin stretch, joint position; slowly adapting.\n- Hair follicle receptors, free nerve endings (crude touch, temperature, pain).\n\n## Receptor (generator) potential\nStimulus opens cation channels (mechanically gated in Pacinian corpuscle) → graded, local, non-propagated depolarisation; **amplitude proportional to stimulus strength**; can summate; no refractory period. When it reaches threshold at first node of Ranvier → action potentials. Shown classically in Pacinian corpuscle (removing lamellae abolishes rapid adaptation).\n\n## Properties of receptors\n1. **Specificity** (adequate stimulus) – **Müller's law of specific nerve energies**: sensation depends on the pathway activated, not on how it is stimulated (pressure on eye → light flashes).\n2. **Excitability/threshold**.\n3. **Adaptation** – decline in response to a constant stimulus (mechanical and ionic mechanisms).\n4. **Coding of intensity**: frequency of APs ∝ stimulus strength (frequency code) and recruitment of receptors (population code). **Weber–Fechner law** – sensation ∝ log of stimulus intensity; Stevens' power law.\n5. **Law of projection** – sensation is referred to the site of the receptor regardless of where the pathway is stimulated (**phantom limb**).\n6. **Coding of modality, location** (receptive field, labelled line), **duration**.\n7. **Receptive field** – area whose stimulation affects a neuron; small in fingertips → fine two-point discrimination (≈ 2–3 mm fingertip vs ≈ 40–60 mm back). Lateral inhibition sharpens localisation.\n\n## Sensory nerve fibres (Erlanger–Gasser / Lloyd)\n- **Aα (Ia, Ib)** – muscle spindle, Golgi tendon organ (fastest, 70–120 m/s).\n- **Aβ (II)** – touch, pressure, vibration (30–70 m/s).\n- **Aδ (III)** – fast pain, cold (5–30 m/s).\n- **C (IV)** – slow pain, warmth, itch (0.5–2 m/s, unmyelinated).\n\n## Applied\nTwo-point discrimination testing (dorsal column/cortical lesions); vibration sense lost early in diabetic neuropathy and B₁₂ deficiency; phantom limb pain; tolerance of clothing touch (adaptation).","q":{"b":["Receptors are transducers; generate receptor potentials.","Adequate stimulus = most sensitive energy form.","Phasic (rapid): Pacinian, Meissner's; tonic (slow): Merkel's, Ruffini, spindle, nociceptors.","Pacinian – vibration ≈ 200–300 Hz.","Meissner's – fine touch, two-point discrimination.","Generator potential is graded and non-propagated.","Müller's law of specific nerve energies.","Law of projection – phantom limb.","Intensity coded by frequency + recruitment.","Weber–Fechner: sensation ∝ log stimulus.","Two-point threshold 2–3 mm at fingertip.","Nociceptors adapt very little."],"n":["Pacinian 200–300 Hz","Two-point 2–3 mm fingertip","Aα 70–120 m/s","C 0.5–2 m/s"],"m":"Rapid adapters **\"PM\"** – Pacinian & Meissner (\"Pass Me quickly\"); slow – Merkel & Ruffini.","v":["Receptor for vibration? – Pacinian corpuscle.","Law explaining phantom limb? – law of projection.","Do nociceptors adapt? – little or not at all."]}},"cn|Pain":{"d":"## Definition\nIASP (2020): \"an unpleasant sensory and emotional experience associated with, or resembling that associated with, actual or potential tissue damage\". Protective; nociception ≠ pain (pain includes perception and emotion).\n\n## Nociceptors\nFree nerve endings; **slowly adapting** (may sensitise – hyperalgesia); mechanical, thermal (> 45 °C; TRPV1 – capsaicin), chemical, polymodal. Chemical mediators: **bradykinin** (most potent), K⁺, H⁺, serotonin, histamine, prostaglandins (sensitise – NSAIDs act here), substance P, ATP.\n\n## Two types of pain\n- **Fast (first, pricking) pain** – **Aδ fibres** (5–30 m/s), well localised, glutamate; **neospinothalamic tract** → VPL thalamus → somatosensory cortex.\n- **Slow (second, burning, aching) pain** – **C fibres** (0.5–2 m/s), poorly localised, substance P + glutamate; **paleospinothalamic tract** → reticular formation, intralaminar thalamic nuclei → widespread cortex, limbic (emotional component).\n\n## Pathway\nFirst-order neuron (DRG) → dorsal horn (**laminae I, II – substantia gelatinosa, V**) → second-order axons cross in anterior white commissure (within 1–2 segments) → **lateral spinothalamic tract** (anterolateral system) → thalamus (VPL, VPM for face via trigeminal) → cortex (S1, S2, insula, anterior cingulate – affective).\n\n## Visceral pain\nPoorly localised, from stretch, ischaemia, chemical irritation, spasm (cutting/burning gut doesn't hurt); carried with sympathetic (and pelvic parasympathetic) afferents; accompanied by autonomic responses, nausea.\n\n## Referred pain\nPain felt at a site distant from the origin, in the dermatome of the same spinal segment – **convergence-projection theory** (visceral and somatic afferents converge on the same dorsal horn neurons) and facilitation. Examples: heart → left arm, inner arm (T1–T4); diaphragm → **shoulder tip (C3–C5)**; appendix → umbilicus (T10); gall bladder → right scapula; kidney/ureter → loin to groin; testis → umbilicus/loin.\n\n## Pain modulation\n- **Gate control theory (Melzack & Wall, 1965)** – large Aβ (touch) fibres activate inhibitory interneurons in substantia gelatinosa, closing the \"gate\" to C-fibre input; basis of rubbing an injury, **TENS**, acupuncture (partially).\n- **Descending analgesia system**: **periaqueductal grey (PAG)** → **nucleus raphe magnus** (serotonin) and locus coeruleus (noradrenaline) → dorsal horn → **enkephalin** interneurons → presynaptic inhibition (↓Ca²⁺ entry, ↓substance P) and postsynaptic hyperpolarisation.\n- **Endogenous opioids** – β-endorphin, enkephalins, dynorphins acting on μ, δ, κ receptors; stress-induced analgesia, placebo effect.\n- Endocannabinoids.\n\n## Abnormal pain\n- **Hyperalgesia** (↑pain to painful stimulus – primary at injury, secondary around it) and **allodynia** (pain from non-painful stimulus) – peripheral and central sensitisation (wind-up, NMDA).\n- **Neuropathic pain** – nerve damage (diabetic neuropathy, post-herpetic neuralgia, trigeminal neuralgia); burning, shooting.\n- **Thalamic (Dejerine–Roussy) syndrome** – thalamic infarct → severe contralateral spontaneous pain.\n- **Phantom limb pain**; causalgia/CRPS.\n- **Congenital insensitivity to pain** (SCN9A) – repeated injuries.\n- **Headache** – brain tissue itself is pain-insensitive; pain from meninges, vessels, sinuses.\n\n## Treatment principles\nNSAIDs (↓prostaglandin sensitisation), opioids, local anaesthetics (Na⁺ channel block – C fibres blocked first), anticonvulsants/antidepressants for neuropathic pain, TENS, cordotomy (historical).","q":{"b":["Pain = sensory + emotional experience.","Nociceptors: free endings, slowly adapting; bradykinin most potent.","Fast pain – Aδ, neospinothalamic, well localised.","Slow pain – C fibres, paleospinothalamic, substance P.","Pain fibres synapse in laminae I, II, V; cross within 1–2 segments.","Lateral spinothalamic tract carries pain and temperature.","Referred pain – convergence-projection.","Heart → left arm; diaphragm → shoulder tip.","Gate control theory – Melzack & Wall, basis of TENS.","Descending analgesia: PAG → raphe magnus → enkephalin.","Hyperalgesia vs allodynia.","Thalamic syndrome – contralateral spontaneous pain.","Brain parenchyma is insensitive to pain."],"n":["Aδ 5–30 m/s","C 0.5–2 m/s","Heat pain > 45 °C","Gate theory 1965"],"m":"**\"PAG-RAM-ENK\"** – PAG → Raphe magnus → Enkephalin interneurons (descending analgesia).","v":["Fibres for slow pain? – C fibres.","Theory behind TENS? – gate control theory.","Pain of myocardial ischaemia referred to? – left arm/chest (T1–T4)."]}},"cn|Spinal reflexes":{"d":"## Reflex arc\nReceptor → afferent neuron → centre (one or more synapses) → efferent neuron → effector. A reflex is an involuntary, stereotyped response to a stimulus. **Monosynaptic** (stretch reflex) or **polysynaptic** (withdrawal).\n\n## Muscle spindle\nEncapsulated intrafusal fibres parallel to extrafusal fibres: **nuclear bag** (dynamic and static) and **nuclear chain** fibres. Afferents – **primary (Ia, annulospiral)** endings: respond to length and rate of change (dynamic); **secondary (II, flower-spray)** endings: static length. Efferent – **γ motor neurons** contract the polar ends → keep spindle sensitive during contraction (**α–γ co-activation**). Spindle signals **length**.\n\n## Stretch (myotatic) reflex\n- Stretch → spindle Ia → **monosynaptic** excitation of α motor neurons of same muscle → contraction; reciprocal inhibition of antagonist (via Ia inhibitory interneuron – disynaptic).\n- **Phasic** (tendon jerks – knee jerk L2–L4, ankle S1–S2, biceps C5–C6, triceps C6–C7, supinator C5–C6) and **tonic** (maintains muscle tone and posture).\n- Central delay ≈ 0.5–1 ms (one synapse). Reflex time of knee jerk ≈ 20–25 ms.\n- **Muscle tone** = continuous partial contraction maintained by the stretch reflex, regulated by γ-motor drive from higher centres (reticulospinal, vestibulospinal).\n- **H-reflex** – electrical equivalent (stimulating Ia fibres; tibial nerve, soleus).\n\n## Inverse stretch reflex (autogenic inhibition)\n**Golgi tendon organ** (in series with muscle; senses **tension**) → **Ib** afferents → inhibitory interneuron → relaxation of same muscle; protects against excessive tension (**clasp-knife** phenomenon in spasticity). Disynaptic.\n\n## Withdrawal (flexor) reflex\nPainful stimulus → polysynaptic → flexion of the limb (**nociceptive, protective**); properties: after-discharge, irradiation, local sign (pattern depends on stimulus site), recruitment, prepotent (dominates other reflexes). **Crossed extensor reflex** – extension of opposite limb to support weight (≈ 200–500 ms delay).\n\n## Other reflexes\n- **Superficial reflexes** (polysynaptic, cutaneous): abdominal (T7–T12), cremasteric (L1–L2), plantar (S1 – normal **flexor**), corneal, anal (S4–S5).\n- **Babinski sign** – extensor plantar (dorsiflexion of great toe, fanning) – **UMN (pyramidal) lesion**; normal in infants < 1–2 years (myelination incomplete).\n- Visceral reflexes (micturition, defecation), scratch reflex, positive supporting reaction.\n\n## Properties of reflexes\nOne-way conduction, reflex delay (central delay ∝ number of synapses), summation, after-discharge, fatigue, facilitation/inhibition, reciprocal innervation (**Sherrington**), final common path (α motor neuron).\n\n## Grading of tendon reflexes\n0 – absent; 1+ – diminished; 2+ – normal; 3+ – brisk; 4+ – very brisk with clonus. Reinforcement (**Jendrassik manoeuvre**) – ↑γ drive.\n\n## Clinical significance\n- **Hyporeflexia/areflexia** – LMN lesion, root or peripheral nerve disease, myopathy (late), spinal shock, hypothyroidism (slow relaxation).\n- **Hyperreflexia, clonus, Babinski** – UMN lesion (loss of descending inhibition, ↑γ activity).\n- **Clonus** – rhythmic contractions on sustained stretch (ankle, patellar) – UMN lesion.\n- Reflex levels help localise spinal segments.","q":{"b":["Reflex arc: receptor → afferent → centre → efferent → effector.","Spindle senses length; Golgi tendon organ senses tension.","Ia – primary (dynamic); II – secondary (static).","γ motor neurons keep spindle sensitive (α–γ co-activation).","Stretch reflex is monosynaptic; reciprocal inhibition disynaptic.","Knee jerk L2–L4; ankle S1–S2; biceps C5–C6; triceps C6–C7.","Muscle tone depends on stretch reflex.","Inverse stretch reflex → clasp-knife.","Withdrawal reflex polysynaptic, prepotent; crossed extensor.","Babinski = UMN lesion; normal in infants.","UMN: hyperreflexia, clonus; LMN: areflexia.","Jendrassik manoeuvre reinforces jerks."],"n":["Knee L2–L4","Ankle S1–S2","Biceps C5–C6","Triceps C6–C7","Knee jerk reflex time ≈ 20–25 ms"],"m":"Jerks **\"1-2 buckle my shoe (S1–2 ankle), 3-4 kick the door (L3–4 knee), 5-6 pick up sticks (C5–6 biceps), 7-8 lay them straight (triceps C7, range C6–C8)\"**.","v":["Only monosynaptic reflex? – stretch reflex.","Receptor for inverse stretch reflex? – Golgi tendon organ.","Significance of Babinski sign? – UMN lesion."]}},"cn|Motor system":{"d":"## Organisation\nHierarchy: **cerebral cortex** (planning, initiation) → brainstem → spinal cord (reflexes, pattern generators) → **final common path (α motor neuron)**. Cerebellum and basal ganglia modulate via thalamus.\n\n## Motor cortex\n- **Primary motor cortex (area 4)** – precentral gyrus; **Betz cells** (layer V); somatotopic **motor homunculus** (inverted; face lateral, leg on medial surface; large hand/face representation); low threshold; controls fine voluntary movement of contralateral side.\n- **Premotor area (6)** – motor programs, posture/orientation; **supplementary motor area** (medial 6) – planning of complex sequences, bimanual; active even when movement is imagined. **Frontal eye field (8)** – voluntary conjugate gaze to opposite side.\n- **Broca's area (44, 45)** – motor speech (dominant hemisphere).\n- Posterior parietal cortex (5, 7) – sensory guidance.\n- Readiness (Bereitschafts) potential precedes voluntary movement.\n\n## Descending pathways\n### Pyramidal (corticospinal and corticobulbar)\nOrigin – ≈ 30% area 4, ≈ 30% areas 6, ≈ 40% parietal (3,1,2,5,7); ≈ 1 million fibres per side; Betz cells only ≈ 3%. Course: corona radiata → **posterior limb of internal capsule** → cerebral peduncle → pons → medullary pyramids → **≈ 80% cross (pyramidal decussation)** → **lateral corticospinal tract** (limb distal muscles, fine skilled movements); ≈ 20% uncrossed → **anterior corticospinal tract** (axial muscles, crosses at segmental level). Corticobulbar fibres to cranial nerve nuclei (mostly bilateral, except lower face (VII) and XII – contralateral).\n### Extrapyramidal (brainstem) pathways\n- **Rubrospinal** – flexor tone of upper limbs (less important in humans).\n- **Medial and lateral vestibulospinal** – extensor/antigravity tone, head and neck posture.\n- **Pontine (medial) reticulospinal** – ↑extensor tone; **medullary (lateral) reticulospinal** – inhibits extensor tone.\n- **Tectospinal** – head turning to visual/auditory stimuli.\nMedial system – axial/posture; lateral system – distal limbs.\n\n## Posture and decerebration\n- **Decerebrate rigidity** (transection between superior and inferior colliculi) – extension of all four limbs, ↑γ activity due to unopposed vestibular and pontine reticular facilitation; abolished by dorsal root section (γ-rigidity). In humans – lesion of midbrain/pons: arms extended and pronated.\n- **Decorticate rigidity** – lesion above red nucleus: flexion of upper limbs, extension of lower limbs.\n- Postural reflexes – tonic neck/labyrinthine, righting reflexes (midbrain), placing and hopping (cortex).\n\n## Upper vs lower motor neuron lesions\n**UMN lesion** (e.g. stroke in internal capsule):\n- Muscles affected in groups; no significant wasting (disuse only).\n- **Spastic** (clasp-knife) hypertonia; exaggerated tendon reflexes, **clonus**.\n- **Babinski positive**; superficial (abdominal, cremasteric) reflexes lost.\n- No fasciculations; contralateral if above decussation.\n- Initial phase may be flaccid (cerebral/spinal shock).\n**LMN lesion** (anterior horn cell, root, nerve – e.g. poliomyelitis):\n- Individual muscles affected; marked **wasting**.\n- **Flaccid** hypotonia; reflexes lost; plantar flexor or absent.\n- **Fasciculations**; reaction of degeneration on electrical testing; ipsilateral.\n\n## Motor unit\nα motor neuron + all muscle fibres it supplies; small units (extraocular ≈ 10 fibres) for fine control, large units (gastrocnemius ≈ 2000) for force. Recruitment by **size principle (Henneman)** – small units first.\n\n## Applied\nCapsular hemiplegia (contralateral face, arm, leg; UMN facial palsy – forehead spared), motor neuron disease (ALS – mixed UMN + LMN signs), cerebral palsy, Brown-Séquard syndrome.","q":{"b":["Area 4 = primary motor cortex; Betz cells layer V.","Motor homunculus – large hand and face areas.","SMA plans complex movements; area 8 = frontal eye field.","Corticospinal fibres pass through posterior limb of internal capsule.","≈ 80% cross in pyramids → lateral corticospinal tract.","Vestibulospinal and pontine reticulospinal ↑extensor tone.","Decerebrate rigidity – γ rigidity, all limbs extended.","Decorticate – flexed arms, extended legs.","UMN: spasticity, hyperreflexia, Babinski, no wasting.","LMN: flaccidity, wasting, fasciculations, areflexia.","UMN facial palsy spares forehead.","Size principle – small motor units recruited first."],"n":["Corticospinal ≈ 1 million fibres","80% cross","Betz cells ≈ 3%","Area 4 precentral gyrus"],"m":"**\"UMN goes UP\"** – tone ↑, reflexes ↑, plantar up (Babinski); LMN goes down.","v":["Where do most corticospinal fibres cross? – pyramidal decussation in medulla.","Type of rigidity in decerebration? – extensor (γ) rigidity.","Key difference UMN vs LMN? – LMN has wasting and fasciculations."]}},"cn|Cerebellum":{"d":"## Functional divisions\n- **Vestibulocerebellum (archicerebellum)** – flocculonodular lobe; connections with vestibular nuclei; **equilibrium, eye movements** (VOR adaptation).\n- **Spinocerebellum (paleocerebellum)** – vermis and intermediate (paravermal) zone; receives spinal proprioceptive input (dorsal and ventral spinocerebellar tracts); controls **muscle tone, posture and execution** of ongoing movements (feedback comparator). Vermis → fastigial nucleus → axial; intermediate → interposed (globose, emboliform) → limbs.\n- **Cerebrocerebellum (neocerebellum)** – lateral hemispheres; cortico-ponto-cerebellar input; → **dentate nucleus** → thalamus (VL) → motor cortex; **planning, timing and sequencing** of skilled movements, motor learning, cognitive functions.\n\n## Cerebellar cortex (3 layers)\n- **Molecular** – parallel fibres, stellate and basket cells.\n- **Purkinje cell layer** – the **only output** of cortex; **inhibitory (GABA)** to deep nuclei.\n- **Granular** – granule cells (the only excitatory cortical neuron; glutamate), Golgi cells.\n- Inputs: **mossy fibres** (from spinal cord, pons, vestibular nuclei; via granule cells → parallel fibres; many-to-one) and **climbing fibres** (only from **inferior olivary nucleus**; one climbing fibre to one Purkinje cell – powerful \"complex spike\"; error signal for **motor learning – long-term depression** of parallel fibre synapses).\n- Deep nuclei (dentate, emboliform, globose, fastigial) receive collaterals of input (excitatory) and Purkinje inhibition → output via superior peduncle mainly.\n\n## Peduncles\nInferior (restiform – mostly afferent: dorsal spinocerebellar, olivocerebellar, vestibular), middle (pontocerebellar, largest, afferent), superior (mainly efferent: dentato-rubro-thalamic; ventral spinocerebellar afferent).\n\n## Functions\n- **Comparator / error correction** – compares intended movement (from cortex – efference copy) with actual (proprioceptive feedback) and corrects.\n- Smoothness, coordination (synergy), **damping** of movements, timing of agonist–antagonist activity (ballistic movements), predictive control.\n- Maintenance of equilibrium and tone; eye movement control.\n- **Motor learning** (adaptation of VOR, skills like typing, cycling).\n- Cognitive and emotional roles (cerebellar cognitive affective syndrome).\n\n## Cerebellar lesions (signs are IPSILATERAL – double crossing)\n- **Ataxia** – broad-based, reeling, drunken gait; truncal ataxia (midline/vermis lesion).\n- **Dysmetria** (past-pointing) – finger–nose test.\n- **Intention tremor** – tremor during movement, increases near target (vs resting tremor in Parkinson's).\n- **Dysdiadochokinesia** – inability to perform rapid alternating movements.\n- **Decomposition of movement**, asynergia.\n- **Rebound phenomenon** (Holmes) – failure of check.\n- **Hypotonia**; **pendular knee jerk**.\n- **Nystagmus** (fast phase to side of lesion).\n- **Scanning / staccato speech** (dysarthria).\n- No paralysis, no sensory loss.\n**Mnemonic DANISH** – Dysdiadochokinesia, Ataxia, Nystagmus, Intention tremor, Scanning speech, Hypotonia.\n\n## Syndromes\n- **Flocculonodular (archicerebellar)** – medulloblastoma in children: truncal ataxia, nystagmus, falls.\n- **Anterior lobe (paleocerebellar)** – chronic alcoholism (vermis degeneration): gait ataxia of legs.\n- **Neocerebellar** – limb incoordination (tumours, stroke, MS).\n- Spinocerebellar ataxias, Friedreich's ataxia.","q":{"b":["Three divisions: vestibulo-, spino-, cerebrocerebellum.","Vestibulocerebellum – balance and eye movements.","Spinocerebellum – tone and execution.","Cerebrocerebellum – planning, via dentate nucleus.","Purkinje cells – only output; inhibitory (GABA).","Granule cells – only excitatory cortical neurons.","Climbing fibres only from inferior olive – motor learning.","Cerebellum acts as a comparator.","Lesion signs are ipsilateral.","Intention tremor vs resting tremor of Parkinson's.","Pendular knee jerk and hypotonia.","Alcohol – anterior vermis; medulloblastoma – flocculonodular.","DANISH signs."],"n":["Purkinje – GABA","4 deep nuclei","3 peduncles","Climbing fibre 1:1 Purkinje"],"m":"Cerebellar signs **\"DANISH\"** – Dysdiadochokinesia, Ataxia, Nystagmus, Intention tremor, Scanning speech, Hypotonia.","v":["Only output from cerebellar cortex? – Purkinje cells.","Source of climbing fibres? – inferior olivary nucleus.","Side of signs in cerebellar lesion? – same side (ipsilateral)."]}},"cn|Basal ganglia":{"d":"## Components\n**Striatum** (caudate nucleus + putamen), **globus pallidus** (external GPe and internal GPi), **subthalamic nucleus (STN)**, **substantia nigra** (pars compacta – SNc, dopaminergic; pars reticulata – SNr, output like GPi). Lentiform nucleus = putamen + globus pallidus. Functionally linked with thalamus (VA/VL) and cortex.\n\n## Circuits\nCortex → striatum (glutamate, excitatory) → output nuclei (GPi/SNr, **GABA – tonically inhibit thalamus**) → thalamus → cortex.\n- **Direct pathway**: striatum (D₁ receptors, GABA + substance P) ⊣ GPi/SNr → **disinhibition of thalamus → facilitates movement**.\n- **Indirect pathway**: striatum (D₂, GABA + enkephalin) ⊣ GPe ⊣ STN → (glutamate) GPi → ↑inhibition of thalamus → **suppresses unwanted movement**.\n- **Dopamine from SNc** (nigrostriatal pathway) excites the direct (D₁) and inhibits the indirect (D₂) pathway → net **facilitation of movement**.\n- Striatal cholinergic interneurons (ACh) balance dopamine.\n- Parallel loops: motor (putamen), oculomotor (caudate – saccades), prefrontal/cognitive (caudate), limbic (ventral striatum/nucleus accumbens – reward, motivation).\n\n## Functions\nSelection and initiation of voluntary movements, suppression of unwanted movements, scaling amplitude/velocity, **planning and programming** of movements, automatic/learned motor patterns (habits, procedural memory), muscle tone, cognition, motivation and reward.\n\n## Disorders – hypokinetic\n**Parkinson's disease** (paralysis agitans): degeneration of dopaminergic neurons of **substantia nigra pars compacta** (Lewy bodies, α-synuclein); symptoms appear after ≈ 60–80% loss.\n- **Resting tremor** (pill-rolling, ≈ 4–6 Hz, disappears in sleep and during movement).\n- **Rigidity** – lead-pipe, or **cogwheel** (rigidity + tremor); both agonist and antagonist (plastic).\n- **Bradykinesia/akinesia** – difficulty initiating, mask-like face, ↓blinking, micrographia, soft monotonous speech.\n- **Postural instability**, stooped posture, **festinant shuffling gait**, freezing.\n- Non-motor – depression, constipation, anosmia, REM sleep behaviour disorder, autonomic dysfunction.\n- Treatment: **levodopa + carbidopa** (dopamine doesn't cross BBB), dopamine agonists, MAO-B and COMT inhibitors, anticholinergics (tremor), **deep brain stimulation** of STN/GPi. Drug-induced parkinsonism (antipsychotics – D₂ block), MPTP toxicity.\n\n## Disorders – hyperkinetic\n- **Huntington's disease** – autosomal dominant **CAG repeat** (huntingtin, chromosome 4) → loss of striatal (caudate) GABA/enkephalin neurons of the indirect pathway → **chorea**, dementia, psychiatric symptoms; anticipation; onset 30–50 years.\n- **Hemiballismus** – violent flinging movements of contralateral limbs due to lesion (often lacunar infarct) of **subthalamic nucleus**.\n- **Athetosis** – slow writhing movements (putamen/kernicterus); **dystonia**; Sydenham's chorea (rheumatic fever); **Wilson's disease** (hepatolenticular degeneration – copper; Kayser–Fleischer ring); tardive dyskinesia; tics/Tourette's.\n\n## Differences from cerebellum\nBasal ganglia lesions – contralateral, involuntary movements and rigidity, tremor at rest; cerebellar lesions – ipsilateral, ataxia, hypotonia, intention tremor.","q":{"b":["Striatum = caudate + putamen; lentiform = putamen + GP.","Output nuclei GPi/SNr tonically inhibit thalamus (GABA).","Direct pathway (D₁) facilitates movement.","Indirect pathway (D₂, via STN) inhibits movement.","Dopamine from SNc favours movement.","Parkinson's – SNc dopamine loss (60–80% before symptoms).","Parkinson triad: resting tremor, rigidity, bradykinesia (+ postural instability).","Cogwheel/lead-pipe rigidity.","Levodopa + carbidopa; DBS of STN.","Huntington's – CAG repeat, striatal loss, chorea.","Hemiballismus – subthalamic nucleus lesion.","Wilson's – copper, lenticular nucleus.","Basal ganglia lesion signs are contralateral."],"n":["Symptoms at 60–80% SNc loss","Tremor 4–6 Hz","Huntington chromosome 4"],"m":"Parkinson **\"TRAP\"** – Tremor, Rigidity, Akinesia, Postural instability.","v":["Neurotransmitter deficient in Parkinson's? – dopamine (nigrostriatal).","Lesion causing hemiballismus? – subthalamic nucleus.","Why carbidopa with levodopa? – blocks peripheral decarboxylation."]}},"cn|Hypothalamus & limbic":{"d":"## Hypothalamus\nSmall (≈ 4 g) part of diencephalon below thalamus, forming floor and walls of third ventricle; the **head ganglion of the autonomic nervous system** and master integrator of homeostasis, endocrine and behaviour.\n\n### Nuclei (key)\n- Preoptic/anterior – heat loss, sleep (VLPO), GnRH.\n- **Supraoptic and paraventricular** – ADH and oxytocin; PVN also CRH, TRH, autonomic.\n- **Suprachiasmatic** – circadian clock (retinal input).\n- **Arcuate (infundibular)** – releasing hormones, dopamine; POMC (satiety) and NPY/AgRP (hunger) neurons respond to leptin, insulin, ghrelin.\n- **Ventromedial** – **satiety centre**; **lateral hypothalamic area** – **feeding (hunger) centre** and thirst; orexin neurons (wakefulness).\n- Posterior – heat conservation/production, sympathetic.\n- **Mammillary bodies** – memory (Papez circuit).\n\n### Functions\n1. **Autonomic control** – posterior/lateral → sympathetic; anterior/medial → parasympathetic.\n2. **Temperature regulation** – anterior (heat loss: sweating, vasodilation – lesion → hyperthermia), posterior (heat gain: shivering, vasoconstriction – lesion → poikilothermia). Set point raised by pyrogens (PGE₂) – fever.\n3. **Food intake** – VMN lesion → hyperphagia, obesity; lateral lesion → aphagia, starvation. Glucostatic, lipostatic (leptin), thermostatic, gut hormone signals.\n4. **Water balance** – thirst (lateral, OVLT osmoreceptors, angiotensin II on subfornical organ) and ADH.\n5. **Endocrine** – releasing/inhibiting hormones via portal system; neurohypophysis.\n6. **Circadian rhythms and sleep–wake** (SCN, VLPO, orexin – narcolepsy when lost).\n7. **Emotional and sexual behaviour** – rage, fear, sexual behaviour (with limbic system).\n8. Defence (fight or flight) responses.\n\n### Disorders\nHypothalamic obesity, diabetes insipidus, hyperthermia, Fröhlich's syndrome (adiposogenital dystrophy), precocious puberty (hamartoma), narcolepsy, Kallmann syndrome.\n\n## Limbic system\n**Components**: limbic lobe (cingulate gyrus, parahippocampal gyrus, uncus), **hippocampus**, **amygdala**, septal nuclei, fornix, mammillary bodies, anterior thalamic nuclei, hypothalamus, orbitofrontal cortex, nucleus accumbens. Broca called it \"limbic lobe\"; MacLean – \"visceral brain\".\n\n### Papez circuit (emotion and memory)\nHippocampus → fornix → **mammillary bodies** → mammillothalamic tract → anterior thalamic nucleus → cingulate gyrus → cingulum → parahippocampal gyrus → entorhinal cortex → hippocampus.\n\n### Functions\n- **Emotion** – expression and experience (with hypothalamus and prefrontal cortex).\n- **Amygdala** – fear, anxiety, emotional memory, aggression; recognition of facial expressions. Bilateral lesion → **Klüver–Bucy syndrome** (placidity/tameness, hypersexuality, hyperorality, psychic blindness/visual agnosia, hypermetamorphosis). Stimulation → rage/fear.\n- **Hippocampus** – formation of new declarative (explicit) memory, spatial memory (place cells); LTP. Bilateral removal (patient **H.M.**) → **anterograde amnesia**.\n- **Reward/pleasure** – mesolimbic dopamine (VTA → nucleus accumbens); self-stimulation experiments (Olds and Milner); addiction.\n- **Punishment centres** – periaqueductal grey, periventricular zones.\n- Motivation, olfaction (rhinencephalon), sexual and maternal behaviour, autonomic responses to emotion.\n- **Sham rage** – decorticate animals with intact hypothalamus show rage to trivial stimuli (cortex normally inhibits).\n\n### Applied\nTemporal lobe epilepsy (déjà vu, emotional auras, automatisms), **Wernicke–Korsakoff syndrome** (thiamine deficiency – mammillary bodies; confabulation, amnesia), anxiety disorders, Alzheimer's (hippocampus early), herpes simplex encephalitis (temporal lobe).","q":{"b":["Hypothalamus = head ganglion of ANS and homeostatic integrator.","VMN – satiety; lateral – hunger and thirst.","Anterior – heat loss; posterior – heat gain.","SCN – circadian clock.","Supraoptic – ADH; paraventricular – oxytocin, CRH.","Arcuate – leptin-sensitive POMC and NPY neurons.","Orexin loss → narcolepsy.","Papez circuit: hippocampus → fornix → mammillary → anterior thalamus → cingulate.","Amygdala – fear; bilateral lesion → Klüver–Bucy.","Hippocampus – new declarative memory (H.M.).","Reward – VTA → nucleus accumbens (dopamine).","Wernicke–Korsakoff – thiamine, mammillary bodies."],"n":["Hypothalamus ≈ 4 g","Lesion VMN → obesity","Lesion lateral → aphagia"],"m":"Klüver–Bucy **\"HHH\"** – Hyperorality, Hypersexuality, Hypermetamorphosis (+ placidity, visual agnosia).","v":["Satiety centre? – ventromedial nucleus.","Lesion causing Klüver–Bucy? – bilateral amygdala (temporal lobes).","Circadian pacemaker? – suprachiasmatic nucleus."]}},"cn|Autonomic nervous system":{"d":"## General plan\nEfferent system to smooth muscle, cardiac muscle and glands; **two-neuron chain** – preganglionic (myelinated B fibres, cell body in CNS) and postganglionic (unmyelinated C fibres, in ganglia). Controlled by hypothalamus, brainstem (vasomotor, respiratory centres), limbic system. Enteric nervous system is often called the third division.\n\n## Sympathetic (thoracolumbar, T1–L2)\n- Preganglionic cells in the **intermediolateral horn**; exit via ventral roots → **white rami** → paravertebral chain ganglia or prevertebral (coeliac, superior/inferior mesenteric) ganglia; postganglionic via **grey rami** to spinal nerves.\n- **Short preganglionic, long postganglionic**; high divergence (≈ 1:20) → widespread \"mass discharge\".\n- Adrenal medulla receives preganglionic fibres directly.\n\n## Parasympathetic (craniosacral)\n- Cranial nerves **III** (ciliary ganglion – pupil, accommodation), **VII** (pterygopalatine, submandibular – lacrimal, salivary), **IX** (otic – parotid), **X** (≈ 75% of all parasympathetic fibres – heart, lungs, gut to splenic flexure); sacral **S2–S4** (pelvic splanchnic – distal colon, bladder, genitals).\n- **Long preganglionic, short postganglionic** (ganglia near/in organ wall); discrete, localised responses.\n\n## Neurotransmitters and receptors\n- All preganglionic fibres – **ACh** on **nicotinic (Nn)** receptors.\n- Parasympathetic postganglionic – **ACh on muscarinic** receptors (M₁ – gastric/ganglia, M₂ – heart (↓HR), M₃ – smooth muscle, glands).\n- Sympathetic postganglionic – **noradrenaline** (α₁, α₂, β₁, β₂, β₃), except **sweat glands** (ACh, muscarinic – sympathetic cholinergic) and some skeletal muscle vessels (historically cholinergic in animals).\n- Co-transmitters: ATP, NPY (sympathetic), VIP (parasympathetic), NO (NANC).\n\n## Effects (sympathetic / parasympathetic)\n- **Eye** – mydriasis (radial muscle, α₁) / miosis (sphincter), accommodation for near vision (ciliary contraction).\n- **Heart** – ↑rate, force, conduction (β₁) / ↓rate, ↓AV conduction (M₂; little effect on ventricular force).\n- **Blood vessels** – constriction of skin/splanchnic (α₁), dilation in skeletal muscle (β₂) / mostly no innervation (except genital erection, salivary).\n- **Bronchi** – dilation (β₂, mainly circulating adrenaline) / constriction and secretion.\n- **GI** – ↓motility, sphincters contract / ↑motility, secretion, sphincters relax.\n- **Bladder** – detrusor relaxes, sphincter contracts / detrusor contracts (micturition).\n- **Male sex** – ejaculation (emission) / erection.\n- **Salivary** – thick scanty / profuse watery.\n- **Metabolism** – glycogenolysis, lipolysis, ↑BMR / insulin release (vagus).\n- **Sweat glands** – sweating (cholinergic sympathetic); piloerection.\nMost organs have **dual, antagonistic** innervation; some only sympathetic (most blood vessels, sweat glands, piloerector, adrenal medulla, spleen, kidney).\n\n## Functional concepts\n- **Tone** – continuous basal activity (vagal tone keeps resting HR ≈ 72 vs intrinsic ≈ 100).\n- Sympathetic – \"**fight or flight**\", catabolic (Cannon); parasympathetic – \"**rest and digest**\", anabolic, conservation.\n- **Denervation hypersensitivity** – ↑sensitivity to transmitters after denervation (receptor up-regulation).\n- Autonomic reflexes – baroreceptor, micturition, defecation, pupillary light reflex.\n\n## Autonomic function tests\nHeart-rate variability with deep breathing (≥ 15/min difference normal), **Valsalva ratio** (> 1.2), **30:15 ratio** on standing, orthostatic BP fall (> 20/10 mmHg = orthostatic hypotension), sustained handgrip (diastolic rise > 15 mmHg), cold pressor test, sweat tests.\n\n## Applied\nHorner's syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis, enophthalmos – cervical sympathetic lesion; Pancoast tumour), diabetic autonomic neuropathy, Raynaud's phenomenon, organophosphate poisoning (SLUDGE + miosis; atropine), atropine poisoning (\"hot as a hare, dry as a bone, red as a beet, mad as a hatter\"), multiple system atrophy, vasovagal syncope, Hirschsprung disease (absent enteric ganglia).","q":{"b":["Two-neuron chain: preganglionic B, postganglionic C fibres.","Sympathetic T1–L2; short pre-, long postganglionic.","Parasympathetic III, VII, IX, X, S2–S4; vagus ≈ 75%.","All preganglionic – ACh on nicotinic.","Parasympathetic postganglionic – ACh on muscarinic.","Sympathetic postganglionic – NA; sweat glands ACh.","Adrenal medulla = modified sympathetic ganglion.","Vagal tone keeps resting HR below intrinsic ≈ 100.","Sympathetic – fight or flight; parasympathetic – rest and digest.","Erection parasympathetic; emission sympathetic.","HRV deep breathing ≥ 15 bpm; Valsalva ratio > 1.2.","Horner's: ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis.","Organophosphate poisoning → SLUDGE; atropine."],"n":["Valsalva ratio > 1.2","Postural fall > 20/10 mmHg","Intrinsic HR ≈ 100","Sympathetic divergence 1:20"],"m":"**\"SLUDGE\"** (muscarinic excess) – Salivation, Lacrimation, Urination, Defecation, GI upset, Emesis.","v":["Transmitter at sympathetic sweat glands? – acetylcholine.","Components of Horner's syndrome? – ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis, enophthalmos.","Which nerve carries most parasympathetic fibres? – vagus."]}},"cn|Sleep & EEG":{"d":"## EEG\nRecording of summated **postsynaptic potentials of cortical pyramidal cells** (dendritic) using scalp electrodes (**10–20 system**), synchronised by thalamocortical circuits. Amplitude ≈ 20–100 µV.\n- **Alpha (α)** – 8–13 Hz; awake, relaxed with **eyes closed**; parieto-occipital. Opening eyes/mental effort → replaced by beta (**alpha block / desynchronisation**).\n- **Beta (β)** – 14–30 Hz; low voltage; alert, eyes open, frontal; ↑ by benzodiazepines.\n- **Theta (θ)** – 4–7 Hz; children, drowsiness, stage N1/N2; hippocampus.\n- **Delta (δ)** – < 4 Hz; high amplitude; **deep sleep (N3)**, infants; in adults awake – brain damage.\n- **Gamma** – > 30 Hz; attention, binding.\nUses: **epilepsy** (spikes; 3-Hz spike-and-wave in absence seizures), sleep studies (polysomnography with EOG, EMG), encephalopathy, coma, monitoring anaesthesia, **brain death** (electrocerebral silence – supportive), evoked potentials.\n\n## Sleep stages (AASM: N1, N2, N3, REM)\n### NREM (≈ 75–80%)\n- **N1** – drowsy, theta, slow rolling eye movements; ≈ 5%.\n- **N2** – **sleep spindles** (12–14 Hz bursts) and **K complexes**; ≈ 45–55% (largest share).\n- **N3** – slow-wave sleep, **delta** > 20%; deepest (highest arousal threshold), ≈ 15–20%; GH secretion peak, **sleepwalking, night terrors, enuresis**; reduced in elderly.\n- Physiology: ↓HR, BP, respiration; muscle tone present but reduced; parasympathetic dominance.\n### REM (≈ 20–25%) – paradoxical sleep\n- EEG resembles wakefulness (**beta-like, desynchronised**, sawtooth waves).\n- **Rapid eye movements**, **atonia** of skeletal muscles (except eye muscles and diaphragm – glycinergic inhibition of motor neurons from pons), **dreaming** (vivid), irregular HR/BP/respiration, penile erection/clitoral engorgement, ↓thermoregulation, PGO waves.\n- Highest proportion in neonates (≈ 50%).\n\n## Sleep cycle\nNREM → REM cycles every **≈ 90 min**; 4–6 cycles per night. N3 predominates in the first half, REM lengthens towards morning. First REM ≈ 90 min after sleep onset (shortened in depression, narcolepsy). Sleep need: adults 7–9 h; newborn ≈ 16–18 h; elderly – fragmented, less N3.\n\n## Mechanisms\n- **Wakefulness** – ascending reticular activating system (RAS): cholinergic (pedunculopontine, laterodorsal tegmental, basal forebrain), noradrenergic (locus coeruleus), serotonergic (raphe), histaminergic (tuberomammillary), **orexin/hypocretin** (lateral hypothalamus – stabiliser) neurons → thalamus and cortex. Lesion of RAS → coma.\n- **NREM** – **VLPO** (ventrolateral preoptic, GABA/galanin) inhibits arousal systems – \"**flip-flop switch**\".\n- **REM** – \"REM-on\" cholinergic pontine neurons; \"REM-off\" monoaminergic (LC, raphe).\n- **Process S** (homeostatic sleep pressure – **adenosine** accumulates; caffeine blocks adenosine receptors) and **Process C** (circadian – SCN and melatonin).\n\n## Functions of sleep\nMemory consolidation (slow-wave – declarative; REM – procedural/emotional), synaptic homeostasis, glymphatic clearance of metabolites (β-amyloid), energy conservation, tissue repair, immune function. Sleep deprivation → impaired attention, mood, glucose tolerance, ↑accidents.\n\n## Disorders\n- **Insomnia** (commonest), **obstructive sleep apnoea** (obesity, snoring, daytime sleepiness, hypertension; CPAP).\n- **Narcolepsy** – loss of orexin neurons: excessive sleepiness, **cataplexy**, sleep paralysis, hypnagogic hallucinations, sleep-onset REM.\n- Parasomnias – NREM (sleepwalking, night terrors) vs REM (nightmares, **REM sleep behaviour disorder** – loss of atonia; precedes Parkinson's).\n- Circadian disorders – jet lag, shift work.","q":{"b":["EEG records summated cortical postsynaptic potentials.","Alpha 8–13 Hz awake, eyes closed; blocked on opening eyes.","Beta 14–30 Hz alert; theta 4–7 Hz; delta < 4 Hz deep sleep.","3 Hz spike-and-wave – absence epilepsy.","N2 – sleep spindles and K complexes; largest share.","N3 – slow-wave; GH peak; sleepwalking, night terrors.","REM – paradoxical: beta-like EEG, atonia, dreams.","Cycle ≈ 90 min; REM increases towards morning.","Newborn REM ≈ 50%.","RAS maintains wakefulness; VLPO promotes sleep.","Adenosine builds sleep pressure; caffeine blocks it.","Narcolepsy – orexin loss, cataplexy.","OSA – snoring, daytime sleepiness; CPAP."],"n":["Alpha 8–13 Hz","Cycle 90 min","REM 20–25%","N2 ≈ 50%","Spindles 12–14 Hz"],"m":"Waves fast→slow **\"BAT-D\"** – Beta, Alpha, Theta, Delta.","v":["Why is REM called paradoxical sleep? – EEG like wakefulness yet hardest to arouse/atonic.","Wave of relaxed wakefulness with eyes closed? – alpha.","Hallmark of stage N2? – sleep spindles and K complexes."]}},"cn|Learning & memory":{"d":"## Learning\nAcquisition of new knowledge or behaviour as a result of experience; memory is its storage and retrieval.\n### Non-associative\n- **Habituation** – ↓response to a repeated harmless stimulus (Aplysia gill withdrawal: ↓Ca²⁺ entry, ↓transmitter release).\n- **Sensitisation** – ↑response to many stimuli after a strong/noxious one (serotonin → cAMP → ↓K⁺ conductance → prolonged AP → ↑Ca²⁺ entry) – Eric Kandel's work.\n### Associative\n- **Classical (Pavlovian) conditioning** – a conditioned stimulus (bell) paired with an unconditioned stimulus (food) evokes the conditioned response (salivation); extinction if not reinforced; cerebellum for motor conditioning (eye blink), amygdala for fear conditioning.\n- **Operant (instrumental) conditioning** (Skinner) – behaviour shaped by reward or punishment.\n- Others – imprinting, observational learning, insight.\n\n## Types of memory\n### By content\n- **Explicit (declarative)** – conscious recall: **episodic** (events) and **semantic** (facts). Depends on **medial temporal lobe (hippocampus, entorhinal cortex)** and diencephalon; stored in association cortex.\n- **Implicit (non-declarative)** – unconscious: **procedural** skills/habits (striatum, cerebellum), priming (neocortex), conditioning (amygdala, cerebellum), non-associative (reflex pathways).\n### By duration\n- **Sensory memory** – milliseconds.\n- **Short-term/working memory** – seconds to minutes; limited capacity (**7 ± 2 items**); prefrontal cortex; electrical/reverberating circuits, post-tetanic potentiation.\n- **Long-term memory** – days to lifetime; needs **protein synthesis** and structural change (new synapses, dendritic spines) – **consolidation** (aided by sleep, emotion – amygdala; disrupted by ECT, concussion – retrograde amnesia for recent events).\n\n## Cellular mechanisms\n- **Long-term potentiation (LTP)** – hippocampal CA1 (Schaffer collateral) synapses: high-frequency stimulation → glutamate on AMPA depolarises → removes **Mg²⁺ block of NMDA receptors** → Ca²⁺ entry → CaMKII, PKC → insertion of more AMPA receptors (early LTP); later CREB-mediated gene expression and new protein synthesis (late LTP). Retrograde messenger NO. Properties – specificity, associativity, cooperativity (Hebb's rule – \"cells that fire together, wire together\").\n- **Long-term depression (LTD)** – cerebellar Purkinje cells, hippocampus; weakening of synapses (forgetting, motor learning).\n- Neurogenesis in dentate gyrus (adult) – debated contribution.\n- Acetylcholine (basal forebrain, nucleus basalis of Meynert) supports attention and memory.\n\n## Amnesia\n- **Anterograde** – inability to form new memories (bilateral hippocampal damage – patient **H.M.** after bilateral medial temporal lobectomy 1953; could still learn motor skills – mirror drawing, proving separate procedural memory). Korsakoff's syndrome (thiamine deficiency – mammillary bodies, dorsomedial thalamus; confabulation).\n- **Retrograde** – loss of memories before the injury (concussion; recent memories lost more – **Ribot's law**).\n- **Alzheimer's disease** – commonest dementia; β-amyloid plaques, tau neurofibrillary tangles; early hippocampal involvement and **loss of cholinergic neurons of nucleus basalis** → anticholinesterases (donepezil); memantine (NMDA antagonist).\n- Transient global amnesia.\n\n## Factors improving learning\nAttention, motivation, rehearsal (spaced repetition), sleep, emotion (moderate stress), active recall (testing effect) – relevant to students.","q":{"b":["Habituation and sensitisation – non-associative (Aplysia).","Classical (Pavlov) and operant (Skinner) conditioning – associative.","Explicit = episodic + semantic; needs hippocampus.","Implicit = procedural (striatum, cerebellum), priming, conditioning.","Working memory ≈ 7 ± 2 items; prefrontal cortex.","Long-term memory needs protein synthesis.","LTP: NMDA receptor (Mg²⁺ block removed) → Ca²⁺ → more AMPA.","Hebb's rule – fire together, wire together.","LTD in cerebellum – motor learning.","H.M. – anterograde amnesia, procedural learning intact.","Ribot's law – recent memories lost first.","Alzheimer's – loss of nucleus basalis cholinergic neurons."],"n":["Working memory 7 ± 2","H.M. surgery 1953","Sensory memory < 1 s"],"m":"Memory types **\"EPIC\"** – Explicit (Episodic, semantic) vs Implicit (Procedural, Conditioning).","v":["Receptor crucial for LTP? – NMDA glutamate receptor.","Structure needed for new declarative memory? – hippocampus.","Transmitter deficient in Alzheimer's? – acetylcholine."]}},"cn|CSF":{"d":"## Formation\nCerebrospinal fluid is secreted mainly (≈ 60–70%) by the **choroid plexuses** of the lateral, third and fourth ventricles (rest from ependyma and brain interstitial fluid). Mechanism – active transport: **Na⁺–K⁺ ATPase** on apical side moves Na⁺ into ventricles, Cl⁻ and HCO₃⁻ follow (carbonic anhydrase), water via **aquaporin-1** – a secretion, not simple filtration.\n- **Rate ≈ 0.35 mL/min ≈ 500 mL/day (≈ 20 mL/h)**; total volume ≈ **130–150 mL** → turned over ≈ **3–4 times a day**.\n- Inhibited by acetazolamide, furosemide; relatively independent of CSF pressure.\n\n## Circulation\nLateral ventricles → **foramina of Monro** → third ventricle → **aqueduct of Sylvius** → fourth ventricle → **foramen of Magendie** (median) and **foramina of Luschka** (lateral) → subarachnoid space and cisterns → around brain and spinal cord. Driven by secretion pressure, arterial pulsations, respiration.\n\n## Absorption\nMainly through **arachnoid villi/granulations** into dural venous sinuses (superior sagittal), acting as one-way valves when CSF pressure exceeds venous pressure by ≈ 1.5 cmH₂O (absorption ∝ pressure). Also perineural lymphatics, glymphatic pathway.\n\n## Composition (compared with plasma)\n- Clear, colourless; SG ≈ 1.005–1.007; pH ≈ 7.33 (slightly lower; poorly buffered → sensitive central chemoreceptor medium).\n- **Protein 15–45 mg/dL** (plasma ≈ 7 g/dL – ≈ 200 times less).\n- **Glucose 50–80 mg/dL (≈ 60–70% of blood glucose)** – always compare with simultaneous blood glucose.\n- Cl⁻ higher (≈ 120–130 mmol/L), Mg²⁺ higher; K⁺, Ca²⁺, HCO₃⁻, urea slightly lower; Na⁺ similar; PCO₂ higher (≈ 48–50 mmHg).\n- Cells **0–5 lymphocytes/µL**; no RBCs, no neutrophils.\n- Pressure (lumbar puncture, lateral recumbent) **≈ 70–180 mmH₂O (5–15 mmHg)**.\n\n## Functions\n- Mechanical cushion and **buoyancy** – brain weight ≈ 1400 g in air but ≈ 50 g in CSF, protects from acceleration injury.\n- Maintains constant ionic environment; removes metabolites (sink), glymphatic clearance.\n- Compensates for changes in intracranial blood volume (**Monro–Kellie doctrine** – brain + blood + CSF = constant within rigid skull).\n- Nutrition; transport of hormones (melatonin, leptin); central chemoreceptor stimulation (via pH).\n\n## Blood–brain and blood–CSF barriers\n- **BBB** – capillary endothelial **tight junctions**, basement membrane, astrocyte end-feet; passes lipid-soluble substances, O₂, CO₂, water (aquaporin-4), glucose (GLUT1), amino acids (carriers); excludes proteins, many drugs, H⁺/HCO₃⁻ (slow), bilirubin (except neonatal – kernicterus).\n- **Blood–CSF barrier** – tight junctions of choroid plexus epithelium.\n- **Circumventricular organs** lack BBB – area postrema (CTZ), OVLT, subfornical organ, median eminence, posterior pituitary, pineal.\n- BBB breaks down in meningitis, tumours, hypertension.\n\n## Lumbar puncture\nAt **L3–L4 or L4–L5** (below the end of the cord at L1–L2 in adults; L3 in infants); Queckenstedt test (historical); contraindicated in raised ICP with mass lesion (risk of herniation).\n\n## CSF in disease\n- **Bacterial meningitis** – turbid, ↑pressure, **neutrophils**, ↑protein, **↓glucose**.\n- **Tuberculous** – ↑lymphocytes, ↑↑protein, ↓glucose, cobweb clot, low chloride.\n- **Viral** – lymphocytes, mildly ↑protein, **normal glucose**.\n- **Subarachnoid haemorrhage** – blood-stained, **xanthochromia** after ≈ 12 h.\n- **Guillain–Barré** – **albuminocytological dissociation** (↑protein, normal cells).\n- Froin's syndrome (spinal block) – yellow, clots spontaneously; multiple sclerosis – oligoclonal bands.\n\n## Hydrocephalus\n- **Obstructive (non-communicating)** – block within ventricles (aqueductal stenosis – commonest congenital).\n- **Communicating** – impaired absorption (meningitis, SAH) or rarely over-secretion (choroid plexus papilloma).\n- **Normal pressure hydrocephalus** – elderly: gait apraxia, incontinence, dementia (\"wet, wobbly, wacky\").\nRaised ICP → headache, vomiting, papilloedema, Cushing's reflex.","q":{"b":["CSF secreted mainly by choroid plexus (active, Na⁺ pump).","≈ 500 mL/day; volume ≈ 150 mL; turnover 3–4×/day.","Circulation: Monro → III → Sylvius → IV → Magendie/Luschka.","Absorbed by arachnoid villi into venous sinuses.","Protein 15–45 mg/dL; glucose ≈ 60% of blood.","Cells 0–5 lymphocytes/µL.","Pressure 70–180 mmH₂O lying.","Brain 1400 g weighs ≈ 50 g in CSF.","BBB – endothelial tight junctions; CVOs lack it.","LP at L3–L4/L4–L5.","Bacterial meningitis: neutrophils, ↓glucose; viral: normal glucose.","GBS – albuminocytological dissociation.","NPH – gait, incontinence, dementia."],"n":["Volume 150 mL","500 mL/day","Protein 15–45 mg/dL","Glucose 50–80 mg/dL","Pressure 70–180 mmH₂O"],"m":"NPH **\"Wet, Wobbly, Wacky\"** – incontinence, gait, dementia.","v":["Main site of CSF absorption? – arachnoid villi.","CSF glucose in bacterial meningitis? – reduced.","Daily CSF secretion? – ≈ 500 mL."]}},"cn|Spinal cord lesions":{"d":"## Key ascending tracts (for localisation)\n- **Dorsal columns (fasciculus gracilis and cuneatus) – medial lemniscus**: fine touch, vibration, proprioception, two-point discrimination; **ipsilateral** in cord, cross in medulla (internal arcuate fibres).\n- **Lateral spinothalamic**: pain and temperature; crosses within 1–2 segments → **contralateral** in cord.\n- **Anterior spinothalamic**: crude touch, pressure.\n- **Spinocerebellar** tracts – unconscious proprioception.\n- **Lateral corticospinal** (descending, already crossed) – ipsilateral UMN signs below the lesion.\n\n## Complete transection\n1. **Spinal shock** (hours–weeks; ≈ 2–6 weeks in humans): loss of all reflexes, flaccid paralysis, loss of sensation below the lesion, loss of vasomotor tone (**hypotension**, especially lesions above T6 – neurogenic shock), urinary retention with overflow, paralytic ileus, loss of sweating and temperature control. Due to sudden loss of facilitatory descending input (reticulospinal, vestibulospinal). First reflex to return – **bulbocavernosus / anal**, Babinski.\n2. **Stage of reflex activity (recovery)** – returning hyperreflexia, **spastic paralysis**, Babinski, flexor spasms (\"paraplegia in flexion\" – complete), **mass reflex** (flexor withdrawal + evacuation of bladder and bowel + sweating), automatic bladder.\n3. **Stage of reflex failure** – with infection, malnutrition – reflexes lost again.\n- Lesion above **C3–C5** – respiratory paralysis (phrenic); cervical lesion → quadriplegia; thoracic → paraplegia.\n- **Autonomic dysreflexia** (lesion above T6) – bladder/bowel distension → massive sympathetic discharge below lesion → severe hypertension, reflex bradycardia, flushing and sweating above lesion; medical emergency.\n- Paraplegia in extension (incomplete lesions – vestibulospinal intact) vs flexion (complete).\n\n## Brown-Séquard syndrome (hemisection)\nAt the level: ipsilateral LMN palsy, band of total anaesthesia (all sensations), sometimes hyperaesthesia above.\nBelow the level:\n- **Ipsilateral** – UMN paralysis (spastic, hyperreflexia, Babinski) and loss of dorsal column sensations (fine touch, vibration, position); vasomotor paralysis.\n- **Contralateral** – loss of **pain and temperature** (starting 1–2 segments below).\n- Crude touch often preserved (both tracts).\nCauses – stab wounds, tumours, MS.\n\n## Other syndromes\n- **Syringomyelia** – cavity in central canal (usually cervical): **dissociated sensory loss** – bilateral loss of pain and temperature in a **cape-like (shawl) distribution** (crossing fibres in anterior white commissure) with preserved touch and proprioception; later LMN wasting of hands (anterior horn), UMN signs below; painless burns. Associated with Arnold–Chiari malformation.\n- **Anterior cord syndrome** (anterior spinal artery occlusion) – bilateral paralysis and loss of pain/temperature; **dorsal columns preserved**.\n- **Central cord syndrome** – hyperextension injury in elderly with spondylosis: arms weaker than legs.\n- **Posterior column lesions** – **tabes dorsalis** (syphilis – dorsal roots/columns: sensory ataxia, **Romberg positive**, lightning pains, Argyll Robertson pupil, Charcot joints), B₁₂ deficiency (**subacute combined degeneration** – dorsal + lateral columns: loss of vibration/position + UMN signs with absent ankle jerks).\n- **Poliomyelitis** – anterior horn cells: asymmetric LMN paralysis without sensory loss.\n- **Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis** – mixed UMN + LMN, no sensory loss.\n- **Cauda equina syndrome** (below L1) – LMN signs, saddle anaesthesia, bladder/bowel dysfunction; vs **conus medullaris** lesion (S3–S5) – early sphincter loss, symmetrical saddle anaesthesia.\n\n## Clinical localisation\nSensory level (dermatomes: nipple **T4**, xiphoid T6, umbilicus **T10**, inguinal L1), motor level, reflexes, sphincter function. **Romberg's sign** – positive in sensory (dorsal column) ataxia, not in cerebellar ataxia.","q":{"b":["Dorsal columns ipsilateral; spinothalamic crosses within 1–2 segments.","Spinal shock: flaccid areflexia, hypotension, retention.","Spinal shock lasts ≈ 2–6 weeks in humans.","Bulbocavernosus/anal reflex returns first.","Mass reflex after recovery from complete transection.","Autonomic dysreflexia in lesions above T6.","Brown-Séquard: ipsilateral motor + dorsal column loss, contralateral pain/temperature.","Syringomyelia: cape-like dissociated loss of pain/temperature.","Anterior spinal artery: dorsal columns spared.","Tabes dorsalis – sensory ataxia, Romberg +.","SACD (B₁₂) – dorsal + lateral columns.","Polio – pure LMN; ALS – UMN + LMN.","Dermatomes: T4 nipple, T10 umbilicus, L1 groin."],"n":["T4 nipple","T10 umbilicus","Spinal shock 2–6 weeks","Phrenic C3–C5"],"m":"**\"C3, 4, 5 keeps the diaphragm alive\"**.","v":["Contralateral loss in Brown-Séquard? – pain and temperature.","Sensory loss in syringomyelia? – dissociated, cape-like pain/temperature loss.","Romberg's sign positive in? – sensory (posterior column) ataxia."]}},"cn|Thalamus & cerebral cortex":{"d":"## Thalamus\nPaired ovoid grey mass of the diencephalon; the **gateway to the cortex** – all sensations except smell relay here. Divided by the Y-shaped internal medullary lamina.\n### Nuclei\n- **Specific sensory relay**: **VPL** (body – medial lemniscus, spinothalamic), **VPM** (face – trigeminal, taste), **LGB** (vision → area 17), **MGB** (hearing → areas 41, 42).\n- **Motor relay**: VA, VL (from basal ganglia and cerebellum → motor/premotor cortex).\n- **Association**: pulvinar (visual association, attention), lateral dorsal/posterior, dorsomedial (prefrontal – emotion, memory).\n- **Limbic**: anterior nucleus (Papez circuit).\n- **Non-specific**: intralaminar (centromedian) and midline – arousal, part of RAS, slow pain; **reticular nucleus** (GABAergic shell – gating, sleep spindles).\n### Functions\nSensory relay and integration (crude awareness of pain, temperature, touch at thalamic level), motor integration, arousal and consciousness, sleep rhythms (thalamocortical oscillation), emotion and memory.\n### Thalamic syndrome (Dejerine–Roussy)\nLesion (posterior cerebral/thalamogeniculate artery) of VPL/VPM → contralateral hemianaesthesia, **spontaneous severe burning pain** (thalamic pain), hyperpathia/allodynia, ataxia, choreoathetosis, \"thalamic hand\", emotional instability.\n\n## Cerebral cortex\n≈ 2–4.5 mm thick; ≈ 16 billion neurons (≈ 86 billion in whole brain). Six layers (neocortex): I molecular, II external granular, III external pyramidal, **IV internal granular (main input from thalamus – thick in sensory cortex: granular cortex)**, **V internal pyramidal (main output – Betz cells; thick in motor cortex: agranular)**, VI multiform. Functional **columns** (Mountcastle). Brodmann mapped ≈ 52 areas.\n\n### Lobes and functional areas\n- **Frontal**: primary motor (4), premotor/SMA (6), frontal eye field (8), **Broca's motor speech (44, 45)**, **prefrontal cortex (9–12)** – executive functions, planning, judgement, personality, working memory, social behaviour. Lesion (Phineas Gage) → disinhibition, poor judgement, apathy, perseveration, frontal release signs.\n- **Parietal**: primary somatosensory (3, 1, 2) with sensory homunculus; somatosensory association (5, 7) – stereognosis; **non-dominant parietal lesion** → contralateral neglect, dressing apraxia; **dominant (angular gyrus, 39)** → **Gerstmann syndrome** (agraphia, acalculia, finger agnosia, left–right disorientation).\n- **Temporal**: primary auditory (41, 42), **Wernicke's area (22)** – comprehension of language; inferior temporal – face recognition (fusiform; prosopagnosia), memory (medial), emotion.\n- **Occipital**: primary visual (17, striate), visual association (18, 19).\n- Insula – taste, visceral sensation, interoception.\n\n### Language and speech\nWernicke's (comprehension) → **arcuate fasciculus** → Broca's (motor programming) → motor cortex. Dominant hemisphere is left in ≈ 95% of right-handers and ≈ 70% of left-handers.\n- **Broca's (expressive, motor, non-fluent) aphasia** – slow, effortful, telegraphic speech; comprehension intact; aware, frustrated; often with right hemiparesis.\n- **Wernicke's (receptive, fluent) aphasia** – fluent meaningless speech (word salad, neologisms), poor comprehension, unaware.\n- **Conduction aphasia** – arcuate fasciculus: poor repetition.\n- **Global aphasia** – both areas.\n\n### Hemispheric specialisation (split-brain studies, Sperry)\n- **Dominant (categorical)** – language, analytical, mathematics, sequential processing.\n- **Non-dominant (representational)** – spatial relations, face recognition, music, emotional tone of speech (prosody), artistic.\n- Corpus callosum connects hemispheres; section → disconnection syndromes.\n\n### Other\nAgnosia (inability to recognise despite intact sensation), apraxia (inability to perform skilled acts despite intact motor power), neuroplasticity (cortical remapping after injury, amputation).","q":{"b":["Thalamus relays all sensations except smell.","VPL – body; VPM – face and taste.","LGB – vision; MGB – hearing.","VA/VL – motor relay from basal ganglia and cerebellum.","Thalamic syndrome – contralateral severe spontaneous pain.","Cortex 6 layers: IV input, V output.","Area 4 motor; 3,1,2 somatosensory; 17 visual; 41,42 auditory.","Broca 44,45 – expressive non-fluent aphasia.","Wernicke 22 – receptive fluent aphasia.","Arcuate fasciculus lesion – conduction aphasia (poor repetition).","Prefrontal – personality, judgement, executive function.","Gerstmann syndrome – dominant angular gyrus.","Non-dominant parietal lesion – neglect.","Left hemisphere dominant in ≈ 95% of right-handers."],"n":["Cortex 2–4.5 mm","≈ 52 Brodmann areas","Left dominant 95% right-handers","Broca 44,45; Wernicke 22"],"m":"**\"Broca's Broken speech, Wernicke's Wordy speech\"**.","v":["Thalamic nucleus relaying face sensation? – VPM.","Area for comprehension of speech? – Wernicke's area (22).","Which layer receives thalamic input? – layer IV."]}}});